  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 358 PM EST Wed Nov 11 2026\n\nPattern Synopsis: Midlevel analysis depicts a shortwave trough \ncurrently digging across Wisconsin will set up and center itself \nover the Northeastern U.S tonight through the rest of the week. At \nthe surface, showers spread across Northern Michigan driven by low \npressure centered over Lake Michigan with an attached cold front \nsweeping to the south. Light disheveled rain is expected to continue \nthrough this evening before strong cold air advection mixes snowfall \ninto the forecast. Northerly flow will persist through Friday and \nSaturday supporting the potential of moderate banding off of Grand \nTraverse Bay as delta T\u2019s remain upwards of 20 degrees celsius. \n\nAforementioned troughing will continue to allow cP air into the \nnorthwoods, keeping highs in the mid 30s to low 40s through \nSaturday. Midlevel troughing progresses towards the Atlantic and is \nquickly replaced by ridging. Subsidence aloft will support surface \nhigh pressure along with mild temperatures building through the \nremainder of the forecast period. Long range guidance hints at \nanother round of active weather later next week, but details still \nremain blurry at this time.\n\nForecast Details: \n\nThursday and Friday: Warm thermal profiles will continue to occupy \nthe region this evening keeping the majority of precipitation \nstrictly as rainfall through Thursday with light snow mixing in. \nWinds begin to back with time as the aforementioned low and cold \nfront break down and merge with a stronger cyclone over the northern \nOhio Valley. Temperatures falling below freezing Thursday night will \nmaterialize lake effect snow showers typically affecting NNE banded \nareas. While impacts remain low for most parts of the CWA, high-res \nguidance depicts strong convergence residing along the northern \nshore of Lake Michigan, creating a moderate band through Grand \nTraverse Bay Friday night. Widespread overnight lows below freezing \nhave continued the overall cooling trend of ground temperatures, \nhowever current soil temperatures remain in the 40s which will limit \nsnow accumulation. Still the better low-level shear for horizontal \nroll convection depicted in CAM's show the potential for \nlocalized accumulations. QPF remains consistent around a quarter\ninch for Grand Traverse, Leelanau, and Northern Wexford County,\nbut if colder air is able to tap in and create higher snow \nratios Friday night into Saturday morning, better banding could \nresult in localized areas to receiving a few inches of \naccumulations. A general inch or so will remain in the forecast \nat this time, but high end chances of snow reaching Advisory \nlevel criteria cant be ruled out for Friday night. \n\nSaturday and Sunday: Surface cyclone over the Northeast CONUS will \ncontinue northerly flow across Michigan this Saturday and continue \nto draw cP air into the region. Ongoing lake effect snow near the \nMichigan coastlines will begin to diminish this Saturday as high \npressure builds to our West. Highs remain in the low to mid 40s this \nSaturday and Sunday as northern stream flow stays south of the state \nbut conditions will feel more pleasant as winds diminish Saturday \nevening and cloud cover begins to break down this Sunday. Otherwise \nno impactful weather is expected this Weekend. \n\nMonday through Wednesday outlook: Upstream 500mb ridging begins to \nbuild through the remainder of the forecast period. Highs build back \ninto the 50s Monday and Tuesday with no precipitation expected. Long \nrange ensembles hint at potential for more active weather around \nThanksgiving with another trough developing upstream. Details on \nimpacts remain blurry at this time, especially with regards to P-\ntypes, but we will continue to monitor its potential as we head \ntowards the end of next week.",
